WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.240
The toolbox, from cheap to expensive.

2
00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:07.064
Think of four tiers, and route documents to the cheapest tier that handles them well.

3
00:00:07.344 --> 00:00:11.568
Text-layer parsers (pypdf and similar) read the embedded characters.

4
00:00:11.848 --> 00:00:15.155
Use them for born-digital, single-column documents.

5
00:00:15.435 --> 00:00:21.451
Cost is near zero, so run them on everything first and use their output to decide what needs more.

6
00:00:21.731 --> 00:00:25.528
Layout-aware parsers run layout models to recover structure.

7
00:00:25.808 --> 00:00:47.227
Docling, started at IBM Research Zurich and now hosted by the LF AI and Data Foundation, parses PDF, DOCX, PPTX, XLSX, HTML, images, and more; recovers reading order, table structure, code, and formulas; runs OCR for scanned pages; and exports Markdown or lossless JSON.

8
00:00:47.507 --> 00:00:52.477
It is MIT licensed and runs locally, which matters for sensitive documents.

9
00:00:52.757 --> 00:01:08.587
Unstructured partitions documents into typed elements (titles, narrative text, tables) with metadata; note that its own docs describe the open-source library as suited to prototyping, with stronger table extraction in its paid API.

10
00:01:08.867 --> 00:01:17.613
These tools turn “a stream of characters” into “headings, paragraphs, and tables”, which the structure-aware chunking in lesson 5 depends on.

11
00:01:17.893 --> 00:01:22.096
OCR engines such as Tesseract turn page images into text.

12
00:01:22.376 --> 00:01:31.464
They are local and cheap but struggle with complex layouts, and they introduce character errors (an “l” read as “1”) that a text layer would not have.

13
00:01:31.744 --> 00:01:34.603
Vision-capable models read the rendered page.

14
00:01:34.883 --> 00:01:46.573
Claude’s PDF support converts each page to an image and also passes the extracted text, so the model sees both; Gemini and OpenAI document inputs work similarly.

15
00:01:46.853 --> 00:01:54.533
They handle charts, handwriting, and messy layouts that defeat rule-based tools, and they can transcribe a page into clean Markdown.

16
00:01:54.813 --> 00:02:03.133
They are also the most expensive tier per page, they have request limits, and, like any model output, a transcription can be fluent and wrong.
